{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/210624-compact-the-bookhounds-of-london","id":210624,"title":"Compact: The Bookhounds of London","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Hunter: The Vigil"],"posts":[{"date":"2014-07-27T16:22:15-07:00","id":210624,"title":"Compact: The Bookhounds of London","content":"(A <i>fantastic</i> little idea originally from <i>Trail of Cthulhu</i>; proper credit where credit is due, this is not my concept, yadda yadda. Enjoy my new favorite bunch of occult criminals.)<br /> <br /> <i>&quot;What you hold in your hands is a genuine excerpt from the Book of Dead Names, scribed by the Mad Arab himself. Price? Oh, I'm sure we can figure something out.&quot;</i><br /> <br /> The financial crisis of the 1930s hit people across the globe, ruining many investors, entrepreneurs, and wealthy families. Collectors of all sorts were forced to sell everything they owned in order to eat and stay afloat, while a number of black-hearted aristocrats turned to the occult to save their prestige and fortunes. It is at this nexus of desperation and despair where the first Bookhounds got their start, as a ramshackle bunch of opportunists pooling their skills in the name of greed, the consequences be damned. Some believe they peddle nothing more than superstitious hogwash to gullible people with too much money, but any Bookhound who has been at the job for long knows better than to write off the secret truths of the world. It doesn't stop them from doing what they do, of course. Nothing could.<br /> <br /> A number of groups have an understanding off the supernatural, and their intent is largely polarized; either they will use it for their own means, or they will take up a Vigil. The Bookhounds take a third path; steal these mystic texts and works of art from those undeserving of them, sell such things (or <i>excellent </i>forgeries) to interested parties for a tidy profit, and try to get far enough away from the mess that inevitably results. There's never a dull day for a Bookhound.<br /> <br /> The first Bookhounds operated out of London, as independent groups spontaneously rising up around local bookshops and collector's auctions, and while they've expanded across the world (Bookhounds can be found just as readily in Boston as they can in Bangladesh), there's still nothing to tie them together other than a common purpose. There's no central headquarters for the Bookhounds and no authority that unites them (indeed, they freely compete with each other for business, and this competition sometimes turns bloody). Even their name - Bookhound - is just an umbrella term for unrelated similar groups; London is simply a hub of such sorts.<br /> <br /> <b>The Enemy</b><br /> <br /> Bookhounds chiefly conflict with parties interested in the same works (tomes, paintings, sculptures, or what have you) that they sell; as such, most of their fighting is actually with Hunters and the mundane authorities. Few look kindly on the spreading of dangerous occult knowledge; the more zealous among those who uphold the Vigil see them as little better than monsters themselves, and will stop at nothing to at least destroy (or take) their hard-earned collections and drive their business out of town.<br /> <br /> Witches often want the same items that the Bookhounds work so hard to steal and sell, and rarely willing to simply purchase them. Others simply take grave offense at &quot;mere mortals&quot; trying to grasp their enlightened mysteries, and will teach a potentially lethal lesson to impetuous types playing with things beyond their ken. It is a bit of cruel irony that the Bookhounds' own buyers often become a problem, attempting rituals or communing with things that shatter their sanity and invade this world. Most Bookhounds will simply duck and run for cover, but a few feel a need to clean up after what they've facilitated (and if they can recover the text to re-sell later, even better).<br /> <br /> Sometimes, the Bookhounds' main enemy is nothing more than the mundane authorities. Crime is a matter of course for a Bookhound - theft, forgery, the occasional bit of violence if someone screws them out of a deal - and rookies to the business often do a stint or two behind bars before getting the hang of things (or meeting an untimely end). Rival Bookhounds will sometimes feud over collections or territory, but most realize it's smarter in the long run to simply pool their efforts and try to gain a monopoly in the area.<br /> <br /> <b>Hunters</b><ul><li>You worked in the stacks at a museum and loved the books that surrounded you. Eventually, it began to worry at you that all this knowledge was simply rotting in archives and private collections; it deserves to be free and in the hands of those who crave it. The break-ins you conduct are nothing shy of your purpose in life, and you wouldn't trade it for the world.</li> <li>You're an honest criminal, and can say without a doubt that no one has ever caught one of your forgeries. Faking painted masterpieces got quite frankly <i>boring </i>after a while, and you hungered for a new outlet; the occult underground offered you new and exciting works to copy, at much higher risk. The pay is damn good, too.</li> <li>You're a witch, or something like it, and saw this as your golden opportunity to find all the supernatural lore you could ever hope to see. The Bookhounds keep you on as an &quot;occult consultant,&quot; tasked with discerning the prizes from the bullshit and dealing with the occasional catastrophe that results from a sale.</li> <li>You don't quite get what it is you and your new friends do, but you're making more money than you ever did doing anything else. The mumbo-jumbo constantly getting talked about goes well over your head, but what doesn't confuse you is how to break a kneecap or which end of a gun to point at the tentacled monster.</li> </ul><br /> <b>Jobs</b><br /> <br /> A proper Bookhound outfit requires a number of strange and unrelated skillsets in order to succeed. Criminals rub shoulders with scholars of literature and students of the occult, and it's little more than greed that holds them together.<ul><li><b>Books</b>: The Books are those with the knowledge that keeps the Bookhounds in business; they know the difference between the various editions and translations of the texts that they broker, and where to buy and sell them. Most are the public face for their cell, appearing at auctions and estate sales with pockets full of cash and dusty tweed jackets.</li> <li><b>Artists</b>: The Artists do the Bookhounds' dirty work, either as muscle, thieves, or forgers. Few understand the gravity of what they're working with until a deal goes tits-up; those with the chops to make it survive and quickly make one hell of a name for themselves.</li> <li><b>Spooks</b>: The Spooks know things that nobody else does, and it's better not to ask where they learned them. Many are occultists, psychics, or former witches, hungry to surround themselves with lore and cultivate networks of new contacts in the supernatural underground.</li> </ul><br /> <b>Status</b><br /> <br /> It's a ruthless world out there, and those that can survive in this field of work for long prove themselves worth keeping around. As clients gain trust in the local Bookhounds, they'll alert them to increasingly interesting sales and leads, and find it increasingly difficult to leave the business.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Remi. she/her. game designer.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/281-atamajakki","name":"atamajakki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar281_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5487"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-28T09:57:54-07:00","id":211379,"title":"","content":"Note: unfamiliar with source content, but that has no bearing on my comment.<br /> <br /> I'm not saying that after reading this I started statting out a forger despite having nowhere to play HtV right now, but...do you think &quot;Forgery&quot; is a valid spec for Crafts?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Currently playing at Fabula Rasa in the Cold Equations venue; planning an Obrimos for Fallen London.<br /> I can also be found at <a href=\"http://darkersolstice.tumblr.com\" target=\"_blank\">http://darkersolstice.tumblr.com</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2192-heu","name":"Heu","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2192_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"131"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-28T14:15:07-07:00","id":211653,"title":"","content":"It's well done though I think it looses something by pushing it into the Hunter mould. I preferred it simply as the result of the antiquarian book trade in a world where there really are magic books and art works. That'd be more a mortals style thing though.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://theonyxpath.com/downloads/Backer%20Badge%20small.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/178-michael","name":"Michael","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar178_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3560"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}